# indices.topic -- the introductions of Compilerpedia's indices. The topic
# index.NAME is printed at the top of `-help NAME`, before the list of the
# index's topics (compilerpedia::renderIndex). COMPILERPEDIA.md describes the
# format.

@topic index.concepts
title: Language concepts
summary: What Botlish's constructs are, the restrictions that matter, and why the design is so.
index: concepts

== ABOUT THIS INDEX
Entry points to Botlish's distinctive mechanisms: values and bindings, types
and refinements, functions and method syntax, imports and modules, declared
errors, contexts, affine values and coroutines, static compile and the test
framework. Each is a short page with the rules that matter, why they are
so, examples the compiler checks, and the related topics to read next.

@topic index.rules
title: Language rules and semantic guarantees
summary: The rules a program must follow, each stated once, with what the compiler reports when one is broken.
index: rules
alias: semantic rules; language rules; list of rules

== ABOUT THIS INDEX
A rule says what is legal and what a legal program is guaranteed. A rule
that a diagnostic enforces names its code; a broad code such as SURFACE
SYNTAX or TYPE covers several rules, and each rule has its own page here
(the code's page lists them under SPECIFIC RULES).

@topic index.proofs
title: What the compiler proves
summary: The facts the compiler establishes about a program, the forms it recognizes, and what it does not (yet) prove.
index: proofs

== ABOUT THIS INDEX
Three kinds of statement, kept apart on every page:

  LANGUAGE GUARANTEE      what Botlish semantics promise, whatever the
                          implementation (an index that designates no
                          element fails with IndexNotFound);
  RECOGNIZED PATTERNS     what this compiler proves, and the program forms
                          its analyses recognize (a counted loop over the
                          same List's length);
  LIMITATIONS             what may be true of a program but is not proven
                          today (an index computed into a local binding).

A limitation is not a language rule: a program the compiler cannot prove is
rejected or must handle an error it cannot raise, and restating it in a
recognized form is the remedy. Start with proof.overview.

@topic index.errors
title: Errors and diagnostic codes
summary: Every diagnostic code the compiler, the runtime and the backends report, with its specific rules.
index: errors
alias: list of errors; all errors; error codes; diagnostic codes

== ABOUT THIS INDEX
An error is a violation of a language or compilation requirement: the
program is rejected (or, at run time, stops). A diagnostic prints its code
in parentheses, e.g. (CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR) or (SURFACE SYNTAX);
`-help CODE` (the whole code, or its last word: -help UNHANDLED-ERROR)
shows its page, and the "help:" line printed under a diagnostic names the
most specific topic for it.

Codes by family:
  CORE SEMANTIC KIND     the program is invalid (the front end), or failed
                         at run time (RANGE, UNCAUGHT-ERROR, NATIVE-ONLY ...)
  SURFACE SYNTAX         the source does not parse; parser codes such as
                         LIST-DESTRUCTURING start the message
  SURFACE MODULE KIND    an import or module file cannot be loaded
  NATIVE ...             the native backend could not compile, link or run
  HIR ..., CORE ...      HIR text, HIR interchange, core IR text, compiler
                         limits and internal contract failures
Warnings are not errors: see -help warnings and -help lint.

@topic index.warnings
title: Warnings
summary: The built-in linter's rules: what each warning detects, why it matters and when the pattern is legitimate.
index: warnings
alias: list of warnings; all warnings; lint rules

== ABOUT THIS INDEX
A warning marks a pattern that may be undesirable in a program that is
valid: the compiler proves the fact it states, and the page explains the
practice behind it. Warnings are on by default and are one global policy
(-warnings default|off|error; there are no per-warning switches): see
lint.policy, and lint.warnings-vs-errors for how errors, warnings and
principles differ.

@topic index.lint
title: The linter
summary: Botlish's warnings as one coherent, built-in linter: its policy, its modes, its rules and the principles they serve.
index: lint

== ABOUT THIS INDEX
Botlish has no third-party lint ecosystem and no configurable rule set: the
compiler's warnings are the linter, on for everyone, so each must be precise
enough that investigating it normally pays. A legitimate exception to a rule
is first evidence that the rule is too broad (principle.lint-precision).
The topics: the policy and modes (lint.policy), errors versus warnings
versus principles (lint.warnings-vs-errors), every warning, and the
principles the warnings serve.

@topic index.principles
title: Programming principles
summary: Recommended practices for Botlish programs, with the reasoning; some are backed by a warning, most are not.
index: principles
alias: recommended practices; coding guidelines; style guide

== ABOUT THIS INDEX
A principle is advice, not a rule: a program that departs from one can be
correct, and only some principles are mechanically checkable (their pages
name the warning that checks them). The reasoning is the point: what a
practice protects, and when it does not apply. New to Botlish? Start with
principle.idiomatic-botlish: the short list of defaults, each linked to the
page behind it.

@topic index.stdlib
title: The standard library
summary: The standard namespaces: their modules, intrinsic operations, functions and types.
index: stdlib

== ABOUT THIS INDEX
A standard namespace NAME is the module stdlib/NAME.bot, the compiler's
intrinsics registered under NAME::, or both; a file uses its members after
`import NAME` (concept.imports), and the root namespace's primitives need no
import. Below are the namespaces; each namespace's page lists its members,
and every member has a page of its own: -help std::list::at, -help
std::str::concat, -help std::test::value. The signatures, errors and backend
availability are generated from the compiler's registry and the library
sources (tools/compilerpedia.tcl generate), so they are what this compiler
accepts. Looking a symbol up never imports or loads anything.

@topic index.backends
title: Backends, targets and the command line
summary: How programs are compiled and run: the four backends, native executables, targets, platforms and the command line.
index: backends

== ABOUT THIS INDEX
Every backend implements the same semantics; they differ in speed, in what
they can do (native-only operations such as printing through io), and in
where they run. Start with backend.overview; the command line is
cli.usage and cli.options.
